\begin{abstract}
Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) in law must trace multi-hop precedent chains and must not present overruled doctrine as current law, yet common evaluators reward entity overlap rather than grounded reasoning. We introduce TERRA (Temporal-Evolution and Reasoning-Trace RAG), an experimental dual-phase GraphRAG architecture. Offline, it builds an Event Evolution Graph over \CorpusTotalOpinions\ Supreme Court opinions connected by \CorpusPrecedentEdges\ directed citation edges, plus a procedural reasoning-trace index. Online, a small-model Traffic Cop router sends single-point lookups down a lighter fast path (retrieval and an entailment check, without graph traversal) and multi-hop questions through citation traversal, and an LLM-prompted entailment grader evaluates retrieved evidence before generation. We evaluate on a \numUniqueQueries-query benchmark against four baselines (\numTotalEvals\ evaluations) using two non-Llama judges, paired Wilcoxon tests and Benjamini--Hochberg correction. TERRA scores higher overall faithfulness ($\TerraOverallFaithfulnessMean$ vs.\ $\HippoRAGOverallFaithfulnessMean$ for the next best system, overlapping 95\% CIs) and answer-only faithfulness ($\TerraAnswerOnlyFaithfulnessMean$, computed on differing answered subsets), and significantly outperforms all three retrieval baselines on Category~B multi-hop reasoning ($\Delta$ from $\CatBTerraVsHippoRAGDelta$ [HippoRAG] and $\CatBTerraVsFlatDelta$ [Flat] to $\CatBTerraVsGraphRAGDelta$ [GraphRAG]; FDR $q<0.05$; Flat CI includes zero), although a no-retrieval Direct LLM scores significantly higher on Category~B ($\Delta=\CatBTerraVsDirectLLMDelta$, $q=\CatBTerraVsDirectLLMAdjQ$; TERRA's Category~B faithfulness is low in absolute terms, $\TerraCatBFaithfulness$). On adversarial Category~D queries TERRA safely handles \TerraCatDSafeHandlingRate\ of traps (\TerraCatDRejectionRate\ explicit refusals, \TerraCatDRefutationRate\ verified refutations) with \TerraCatDFatalErrors\ fatal errors, but refuses \TerraCatAFalseRefusals\ Category~A and \TerraCatBFalseRefusals\ Category~B queries, at \TerraLatencyMedianSeconds~s median latency. On \ObscCohortSize\ obscure overruled precedents, Direct LLM safe handling falls to \ObscDirectSafePct\ versus \ObscFullSafePct\ for full TERRA, but TERRA without curated overruling labels drops to \ObscUnlabeledSafePct, not significantly different from flat retrieval ($p\,\ObscPrimaryMcNemarPRel$); safety depends on curated annotations. Absent licensed attorney evaluation, human review remains an essential deployment prerequisite.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
\textcolor{black}{\textit{Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Large Language Models, Temporal Knowledge Graph, Agentic Reasoning, Semantic Entailment, Explainable AI, Temporal Leakage Evaluation}}
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:Introduction}
Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) decouples the parametric memory of Large Language Models (LLMs) from external knowledge bases, enabling factual grounding and source attribution without continuous retraining. In high-stakes settings, where errors and hallucinations carry real consequences, the fidelity of the retrieved context is a strict operational requirement, and naive ``retrieve-then-generate'' pipelines over static vector databases and fixed-length chunks have proven structurally inadequate.

\subsection{Summary of Contributions}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Dual-Index Architecture:} a judicial GraphRAG corpus cross-linking an EEG of \CorpusTotalOpinions\ SCOTUS decisions (\CorpusPrecedentEdges\ citation edges) with a node-level procedural trace scaffold (one five-step template per case, with no case-specific issue, test or holding) and no synthetic cases.
    \item \textbf{Traffic Cop Routing with Coarse Domain Pre-filtering:} an SLM-driven complexity router with an optional coarse proximity flag ($d_{L2} < 1.3$ + Title Match), calibrated offline as a scale-dependent domain-boundary signal; in the canonical run this flag terminated no query before retrieval (Sections~\ref{subsec:computational-budget} and~\ref{subsec:firewall-analysis}).
    \item \textbf{NLI-Based Entailment Evaluation and XAI Attribution:} an LLM-prompted entailment grader that evaluates retrieved evidence and gates generation, with reasoning traces and citation provenance; its interception reliability is not validated beyond a 5-query pilot ablation.
    \item \textbf{Leakage Safeguards and Audits:} safeguards against temporal precedent inversion, benchmark retrieval contamination and cross-domain feature bleeding, with mixed results in the leakage probe.
    \item \textbf{Empirical Evaluation:} across \numTotalEvals\ evaluations (\numUniqueQueries\ queries $\times$ \numComparativePipelines\ pipelines, $T=0.0$) with paired Wilcoxon tests, Cohen's $d_z$ and 95\% bootstrap CIs, TERRA has the highest Answer-Only Faithfulness among RAG architectures ($\TerraAnswerOnlyFaithfulness$ vs.\ HippoRAG's $\HippoRAGAnswerOnlyFaithfulness$), outperforms Flat Vector RAG ($\Delta = \CatBTerraVsFlatDelta$, $p = \CatBTerraVsFlatWilcoxonP$, $d_z = \CatBTerraVsFlatDz$) and HippoRAG ($\Delta = \CatBTerraVsHippoRAGDelta$, $p = \CatBTerraVsHippoRAGWilcoxonP$, $d_z = \CatBTerraVsHippoRAGDz$) on Category~B, delivers $\TerraCatDSafeHandlingRate$ adversarial safe handling ($\TerraCatDSafeHandling$: \TerraCatDRejection\ refusals, \TerraCatDRefutations\ refutations) with \TerraCatDFatalErrors\ fatal errors vs.\ $\BaselineFatalMin$--$\BaselineFatalMax$ for baselines, and incurs \TerraCatAFalseRefusals\ false refusals on Category~A (\TerraCatBFalseRefusals\ on B). Direct LLM scores higher on Category~B ($\Delta=\CatBTerraVsDirectLLMDelta$), and the Flat margin's bootstrap CI includes zero. Evaluations rely on automated LLM judges without licensed attorneys, making human review an essential prerequisite (Section~\ref{subsec:limitations}).
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related-work}
Retrieval-Augmented Generation \cite{lewis2020retrieval, guu2020realm} decoupled parametric memory from external knowledge, but standard RAG struggles with knowledge drift and multi-step reasoning. Graph-based architectures structure retrieved evidence: GraphRAG \cite{edge2024graphrag} builds hierarchical entity-relation communities for global summarization, and Think-on-Graph \cite{sun2024thinkongraph} runs iterative LLM-guided reasoning over knowledge graphs. In temporal domains, Liu and Cao \cite{liu2026raglearningunderstandinglimits} introduced a continuous-knowledge-drift benchmark and Chronos, a time-aware method organizing evidence into an Event Evolution Graph (EEG); Zhang et al. \cite{zhang2025respectingtemporalcausalconsistencyentityevent} proposed E2RAG for temporal-causal consistency across entity-event subgraphs; and Zhu et al. \cite{zhu2025rightanswerrighttime} developed STAR-RAG for time-sensitive rule graph summarization. In law, Chen et al.~\cite{chen2026legalgraphrag} proposed LegalGraphRAG, a Researcher, Auditor and Adjudicator multi-agent system over a hierarchical legal graph, and de Martim~\cite{demartim2025satgraphrag} introduced SAT-Graph RAG to trace diachronic statutory norm versions across constitutional amendments. These systems organize sources by abstraction level or model formal amendment events, whereas common-law systems also require tracking how precedent evolves across citation networks, where earlier decisions remain presumptive authority unless distinguished, limited or overruled; and static graphs retrieve structure but lack the procedural logic for multi-hop reasoning over evolving precedents.

