\subsubsection{Procedural Reasoning Trace Synthesis and Vector Serialization}
Each case is paired with a node-level Structured Legal Reasoning Trace (SLRT), which in the evaluated index is a fixed scaffold built from Harvard Caselaw Access Project (CAP) metadata:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Curated Landmark Prototypes ($N=\TraceCuratedCount$):} during early development, \TraceCuratedCount\ landmark decisions received curated traces generated by instruction-tuned models (\texttt{gemini-3.1-flash-lite} baseline; \texttt{mistral.ministral-3-8b-instruct} variants for benchmark seeds) at $T=0.0$, capped at 150 words and following a five-point schema (constitutional question, governing provisions and standard of review, holding, precedent connection, longitudinal significance). They are not in the evaluated index.
    \item \textbf{Deterministic Procedural Substrate ($N=\TraceProceduralCount$, \TraceProceduralPct):} all \TraceProceduralCount\ opinions are serialized by a procedural function (\texttt{build\_structured\_trace}) from the CAP case title (\texttt{name\_abbreviation}), decision year (\texttt{decision\_date}) and a domain phrase. Every node receives the same five-step template (legal question, governing precedent, factual record, harmonizing doctrines, holding), with no case-specific issue, procedural history, test or holding. The domain phrase differs from the node's \texttt{category} field for 18{,}247 of 20{,}000 nodes (19{,}298 read ``Civil Rights Equal Protection'', although 1{,}153 nodes carry that category). The index thus holds no LLM-generated trace text but supplies no case-specific reasoning to the generator.
\end{enumerate}

Each vector document serializes the title header, the SLRT (under the legacy key \texttt{"Thinking Trace:"}) and an opinion preamble snippet:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\text{Document} = \; & \texttt{\{title\} (\{year\})} \\
& \oplus \; \texttt{"\textbackslash nThinking Trace: \{trace\}"} \\
& \oplus \; \texttt{"\textbackslash nOpinion Snippet: \{text\_snip[:1000]\}"}
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:trace_doc}
\end{equation}
All \CorpusTotalOpinions\ documents are embedded with Sentence-Transformers (\texttt{all-MiniLM-L6-v2}~\cite{reimers2019sentencetransformers}, \TraceEmbeddingDimensions\ dimensions, normalized Euclidean $L_2$) and indexed in a local ChromaDB \texttt{PersistentClient} (\texttt{dataset\_20k\_cap/vector\_db\_20k}), giving an aligned dual-index architecture.

\subsubsection{Traffic Cop Router and Multi-Tier Domain Boundary Filtering}
Each query $q$ is first classified by the Traffic Cop Router, powered by Google Gemma 3 12B Instruction-tuned (\texttt{\RouterModel}) at $T=0.0$ under structured Pydantic validation (\texttt{RoutingDecision}):
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\text{Route}(q) = \begin{cases} 
\text{EASY}, & \text{if } \text{Low Complexity} \land \text{In-Domain} \\
\text{HARD}, & \text{if } \text{High Complexity} \lor \text{Out-of-Domain}
\end{cases}
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:routing}
\end{equation}
EASY queries (e.g., decision years or case authors) skip multi-hop expansion and go to the Grounded Fast Path, a $\AblationRouterLatencySlowdownFactor\times$ speedup in the pilot ablation (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}) that also avoids context bloat from tangential citing precedents. Evaluated standalone (Section~\ref{subsec:router-standalone-eval}), the router reaches $\RouterAccuracy$ accuracy and $\RouterMacroFOne$ macro-$F_1$ on the \numUniqueQueries-query suite.

To limit hallucination on out-of-domain topics without static allowlists, TERRA applies a three-layer \textbf{Domain Boundary Safety Framework}:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Tier 1, Lexical Title Matching:} checks whether $q$ contains an exact title of an indexed landmark case from the EEG.
    \item \textbf{Tier 2, Optional Geometric Pre-Filter (Scale-Dependent):} compares the L2 distance of the nearest ChromaDB trace with an empirical threshold:
    \begin{equation}
    d_{L2}(q, v_{\text{nearest}}) < 1.3
    \label{eq:l2threshold}
    \end{equation}
    Embedding distance does not prove legal validity or domain membership; it is an optional coarse pre-filter to conserve compute on obvious out-of-domain inputs. In the \CorpusTotalOpinions-case index (Table~\ref{tab:computational_budget}, Panel~B) in-domain queries cluster tightly (mean $0.365 \pm 0.082$, max $0.509$), so $\tau=1.30$ gives zero in-domain false refusal (\TerraGateFPRTauOneThreeZero, \TerraGateFPRCountTauOneThreeZero) while flagging \TerraGateTPRTauOneThreeZero\ (\TerraGateTPRCountTauOneThreeZero) of out-of-domain probes by distance; in the canonical run no query was terminated before retrieval.
    \item \textbf{Tier 3, Semantic Entailment Verification:} because proximity cannot verify authority or prevent misapplication, the NLI Smart Grader checks the retrieved subgraph, and if entailment fails across two expansion attempts, generation ends in a deterministic safety refusal. In the canonical run, 81 of the 139 refusals followed an empty retrieved context (74 of the 75 Category~C probes) and 58 a non-empty one (Section~\ref{subsec:firewall-analysis}).
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{Multi-Hop Citation Graph Traversal}
For HARD queries, retrieval combines vector and graph traversal. \textbf{Seed retrieval:} ChromaDB returns the top-$k$ ($k=2$) trace vectors as seed nodes $V_{\text{seed}}$, used only for coarse seed discovery; precedent discovery then follows citation-graph traversal and holding injection (Section~\ref{subsec:retrieval-quality}), which insulates synthesis from seeding errors: dense seeding alone reaches $\CatBFlatMacroNodeRecall$ node recall and $\CatBFlatFullPathRecall$ path recovery, whereas two-hop traversal lifts these to $\CatBTerraMacroNodeRecall$ (\TraceTopologicalAbsLift, \TraceTopologicalRelMultiplier) and $\CatBTerraFullPathRecall$ (\TracePathAbsLift, \TracePathRelMultiplier). In the canonical run Category~C showed \CatCTerraCleanRefusals\ clean refusals (74 of 75 probes had an empty context) and Category~D \TerraCatDSafeHandlingRate\ safe handling. \textbf{BFS with hub capping:} from $V_{\text{seed}}$, BFS traverses predecessors and successors to depth $d=2$:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{C}(q) = V_{\text{seed}} \cup \bigcup_{v \in V_{\text{seed}}} \text{BFS}(v, G_{\text{EEG}}, d=2)
\label{eq:bfs}
\end{equation}
Hub expansion is capped (degree $>50$) to prevent super-hub flooding, and node summaries and trajectories are compiled chronologically into the evidence block $\mathcal{C}(q)$.

\subsubsection{NLI Smart Grader and Self-Correction Expansion Loop}
\label{subsubsec:terra-nli-grader}
Before generation, $\mathcal{C}(q)$ is evaluated by the NLI Smart Grader, a structured-prompt call to \texttt{databricks-meta-llama-3-3-70b-instruct}~\cite{dubey2024llama3} ($T=0.0$) given the query and context and instructed to return JSON with \texttt{entails} (boolean), \texttt{confidence\_score} (float) and \texttt{reasoning} (string). This allows single-call verification without a separate NLI cross-encoder (e.g., DeBERTa~\cite{he2023debertav3}), while routing stays with the 12B Gemma SLM. No labeled validation set was built for the verifier; the only empirical evidence of its role is the pilot ablation, in which removing the gate (Ablation~2, Table~\ref{tab:ablation}) lowers adversarial safety rejection from \AblationFullCatDSafetyRate\ to \AblationTwoCatDSafetyRate, which is consistent with the gate contributing to safety but not a validation of its accuracy. False non-entailment (insufficient context passed to the generator) is the principal unquantified failure mode (Section~\ref{subsec:limitations}). If \texttt{entails == True}, the context goes to the generator. If \texttt{entails == False}, a \textbf{Self-Correction Expansion Loop} widens retrieval ($k \leftarrow k + 2$), re-queries ChromaDB and re-runs BFS. If entailment still fails after two expansions, the \textbf{Safety Refusal Firewall} halts generation with a deterministic refusal: \textit{``I apologize, but I do not have sufficient validated legal context in my databases to answer this query accurately without risking hallucination.''}

\subsubsection{Data Leakage Mitigation Framework}
\label{subsubsec:data-leakage}
TERRA applies four safeguards against leakage. (1)~\textbf{Temporal directionality:} an earlier case cannot cite later jurisprudence, so forward \texttt{CITES} edges require $t(v_{\text{cite}}) \ge t(v_{\text{cited}})$, and retrospective invalidation uses CRS-derived \texttt{OVERRULES} relations (Section~\ref{subsec:phase1-ingestion}) rather than unrestricted backward edges, preventing the retriever from projecting modern standards onto historical inquiries. (2)~\textbf{Vector-store isolation:} benchmark queries, rubrics and reference answers are segregated from the index, which holds only opinion text and traces. (3)~\textbf{Cross-domain bleed prevention:} title matching and the proximity flag (which terminated no query in the canonical run) are coarse first-line signals, and NLI verification is designed to suppress non-entailed subgraphs before synthesis. (4)~\textbf{Temporal parametric containment:} to limit post-$T$ doctrine entering historical answers, traversals and retrieved corpora are bounded by provenance timestamps ($\text{KnowledgeProvenance} \le T$), and candidate generations $\hat{y}_{\text{candidate}}$ pass an entailment gate against contemporaneous evidence before release. In the leakage probe (Section~\ref{subsec:temporal-leakage}) the gate made six interventions, but its bulk leakage reduction was not statistically established.

\subsubsection{Explainable AI (XAI) and Topological Provenance}
\label{subsubsec:xai-provenance}
TERRA provides four explainability layers: (1)~\textbf{graph traversal provenance}, an explicit citation path $\mathcal{P} = \langle v_1, e_1, v_2, \dots, v_k \rangle$ from landmark anchor to modern precedent behind each answer; (2)~\textbf{hyperlinked attribution}, in which cited cases with indexed identifiers become relative markdown links (e.g., \texttt{[Brown v. Board of Education](/case/347\_0483-01)}); across benchmark completions TERRA emitted \TerraTotalMarkdownLinks\ links with a \TerraMarkdownLinkResolutionRate\ resolution rate (\TerraResolvedMarkdownLinks/\TerraTotalMarkdownLinks) and \TerraMarkdownLinkFidelityRate\ title fidelity (Section~\ref{subsec:citation-correctness}, Table~\ref{tab:citation_eval}), while the baselines emit none; (3)~\textbf{entailment transparency}, structured justifications (\texttt{faithfulness\_reasoning}, \texttt{confidence\_score}); and (4)~\textbf{routing transparency}, reasoning strings for the EASY or HARD decision.

\subsubsection{Grounded Answer Generation}
When context passes NLI grading, \texttt{databricks-meta-llama-3-3-70b-instruct}~\cite{dubey2024llama3} generates the answer under a strict grounding instruction with greedy decoding:
\begin{equation}
T = 0.0
\label{eq:temperature}
\end{equation}
Hosted inference APIs may still show minor output variability at $T=0.0$.

\subsection{Implementation Details}
\label{subsec:implementation-details}
The prototype is written in Python 3.11 using \texttt{openai}, \texttt{chromadb}, \texttt{networkx} and \texttt{fastapi}, served via a FastAPI/Uvicorn endpoint with an in-memory query cache; telemetry (routing latency, NLI confidence, firewall status) is logged to \texttt{terra\_telemetry\_traces.json}.

\subsection{Corpus Statistics and Doctrinal Taxonomy}
\label{subsec:corpus-stats}
The knowledge base holds \CorpusTotalOpinions\ substantive opinions connected by \CorpusPrecedentEdges\ directed citation edges across \CorpusTemporalSpan\ (Volumes 50--448) in seven doctrinal categories (Table~\ref{tab:corpus_stats}). Each opinion receives exactly one primary domain from a deterministic rule-based taxonomy with an exact \IssueTwentyTwoTotalLexiconPhrases-phrase lexicon over six substantive domains: Substantive Private Law (\IssueTwentyTwoSubstantivePrivateLawPhrases\ phrases, \IssueTwentyTwoSubstantivePrivateLawCorpus\ opinions, \IssueTwentyTwoSubstantivePrivateLawCorpusPct), Administrative Law and Federal Courts (\IssueTwentyTwoAdminLawPhrases, \IssueTwentyTwoAdminLawCorpus, \IssueTwentyTwoAdminLawCorpusPct), Criminal Procedure (\IssueTwentyTwoCriminalProcedurePhrases, \IssueTwentyTwoCriminalProcedureCorpus, \IssueTwentyTwoCriminalProcedureCorpusPct), Commerce Clause and Economic Regulation (\IssueTwentyTwoCommerceClausePhrases, \IssueTwentyTwoCommerceClauseCorpus, \IssueTwentyTwoCommerceClauseCorpusPct), Civil Rights and Equal Protection (\IssueTwentyTwoCivilRightsPhrases, \IssueTwentyTwoCivilRightsCorpus, \IssueTwentyTwoCivilRightsCorpusPct) and First Amendment (\IssueTwentyTwoFirstAmendmentPhrases, \IssueTwentyTwoFirstAmendmentCorpus, \IssueTwentyTwoFirstAmendmentCorpusPct). The seventh domain, General Constitutional and Statutory (\IssueTwentyTwoGeneralConstitutionalCorpus\ opinions, \IssueTwentyTwoGeneralConstitutionalCorpusPct), is an unkeyed residual fallback assigned only when an opinion has no keyword hit in any lexicon; positive-score ties resolve by dictionary insertion order and never to the fallback. Opinions also carry secondary multi-label doctrinal tags.

\subsection{Graph Topology Analysis}
\label{subsec:graph-topology}
The EEG is sparse with high-degree authority hubs and a heavy-tailed degree distribution, as in judicial citation networks. The largest weakly connected component (WCC) spans \CorpusLargestComponentSize\ of \CorpusTotalOpinions\ nodes (\CorpusLargestWccPercent), so multi-hop paths remain navigable across eras (density \CorpusGraphDensity, mean degree \CorpusMeanNodeDegree, maximum degree \CorpusMaxDegree\ at \CorpusMaxDegreeCase, average clustering \CorpusAverageClustering, largest strongly connected component \CorpusLargestSccSize\ nodes). Its diameter of \CorpusGraphDiameter\ hops and average shortest path of \CorpusAvgShortestPath\ hops indicate compact reachability, consistent with a BFS depth of $d=2$ capturing dense local citation neighborhoods without flooding the context.

\subsection{Hub Node Centrality Analysis}
\label{subsec:hub-analysis}
Betweenness centrality, $C_B(v) = \sum_{s \neq v \neq t} \sigma_{st}(v)/\sigma_{st}$ (with $\sigma_{st}$ the number of shortest $s$--$t$ paths and $\sigma_{st}(v)$ those through $v$), identifies structural bridge cases. The top bridges are \textit{Simpson v. Shepard} ($C_B = 0.0043$), \textit{Fay v. Noia} ($0.0036$), \textit{Ashwander v. Tennessee Valley Authority} ($0.0033$), \textit{Crowell v. Benson} ($0.0032$) and \textit{Dahnke-Walker Milling Co. v. Bondurant} ($0.0032$), all major federal constitutional precedents (Fig.~\ref{fig:hub_centrality}). High-degree hubs include \CorpusMaxDegreeCase\ (degree \CorpusMaxDegree), \textit{Baker v. Carr} (245), \textit{Crowell v. Benson} (213), \textit{Nebbia v. New York} (211) and \textit{Fay v. Noia} (204), which link disparate doctrinal lines.

