\subsection{Benchmark Suite Design}
\label{subsec:benchmark-design}
The evaluation suite comprises \numUniqueQueries\ unique queries evaluated across \numComparativePipelines\ pipelines (\numTotalEvals\ evaluations; dates and model identifiers are given under Multi-Run Provenance below), plus a foundational $N=\numPilotQueries$ pilot ablation set (\numPilotRepeatedRuns\ repeated trials per query, \numPilotEvals\ executions across four configurations), across four categories isolating factual accuracy, multi-hop reasoning, domain safety and adversarial robustness. Temperature $T{=}0.0$ was used throughout but does not guarantee identical outputs across runs (Section~\ref{subsec:limitations}). Faithfulness and ROUGE-L are computed on the generative Categories~A and~B, and safety on Categories~C and~D:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Category A --- Single-Point Factual:} single-fact queries (decision years, party names) scored against researcher-constructed reference answers with ROUGE-L.
    \item \textbf{Category B --- Multi-Hop Evolutionary:} multi-decade precedent-tracking queries scored against researcher-constructed reference answers with ROUGE-L. To test non-trivial traversal while reflecting the hub topology of citation networks, all chains span 3--5 nodes (2--4 hops): 63 three-node, 32 four-node and 5 five-node arcs, with no 2-node pairs. Category~B gold paths derive from the EEG's own citations (circularity risk), and Categories~A and~B use three and one question templates.
    \item \textbf{Category C --- Out-of-Context Safety:} 75 queries ($N=15$ each) on topics absent from indexed Supreme Court law (\CorpusTemporalSpan): natural sciences, foreign and treaty law, state and municipal ordinances, post-2020 software and culture, and corporate accounting. With no controlling precedent, the required behavior is refusal.
    \item \textbf{Category D --- Adversarial Deception:} queries pairing real in-domain case titles with fabricated premises or temporal falsehoods (e.g., governing the 1905 \textit{Lochner} ruling by the 1938 Fair Labor Standards Act), built to trick entity-matching routers. A safe response is an explicit refusal or a verified refutation of the false premise citing authentic authority.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Comparative Baselines}
\label{subsec:baselines}
TERRA is benchmarked against four representative baseline architectures spanning parametric and retrieval-augmented paradigms: (1)~\textbf{Direct LLM (No RAG)}, where query $q$ is passed directly to the generator without retrieval, evaluating raw parametric knowledge and un-gated hallucination risks; (2)~\textbf{Flat Vector RAG}, where ChromaDB retrieves top-$k$ reasoning traces by semantic embedding similarity ($n_{\text{results}}=2$) and passes them directly to the generator without citation graph traversal or NLI entailment grading; (3)~\textbf{GraphRAG-Adapted (Louvain implementation)}, adapting Microsoft GraphRAG \cite{edge2024graphrag} by partitioning opinion entities into Louvain graph communities ($Q=\IssueTwentyThreeGraphRAGModularity$) and generating responses from local subgraph plus parent community summaries, omitting directed chronological precedent edges; and (4)~\textbf{HippoRAG-Adapted}, adapting hippocampal memory indexing \cite{bernal2024hipporag} by constructing a symmetric bipartite citation graph (\IssueTwentyThreeHippoRAGNonZeroEntries\ non-zero entries) and executing Personalized PageRank (PPR) associative diffusion seeded from query entity mentions, without typed directional citation constraints or downstream NLI self-correction gates.

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}
\label{subsec:eval-metrics}
System performance is evaluated across five dimensions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Faithfulness ($0.0$--$1.0$):} the mean of two LLM judges \cite{zheng2023judging}, \texttt{databricks-qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct} (Judge~1) and \texttt{databricks-gpt-oss-120b} (Judge~2), each scoring a five-point Likert rubric mapped to $(L-1)/4$. Neither is the Llama-3.3-70B model that serves as generator, NLI grader and Category~D verifier.
    \item \textbf{Relevance ($0.0$--$1.0$):} scored by the two judges for query-answer directness and structural completeness.
    \item \textbf{ROUGE-L F1 and Recall (supplementary):} computed for Categories~A and~B against researcher-constructed reference answers by deterministic tokenized LCS matching, an LLM-independent lexical check. Reference claims were cross-referenced against public sources (Cornell LII, Oyez, CRS \textit{Constitution Annotated}) without independent legal certification. Exact n-gram matching cannot distinguish active doctrine from overruled precedent, penalizes paraphrase and detailed analysis via candidate length, and scores refusals as zero; the modest judge--ROUGE correlation ($r=\JudgePearsonR$; Section~\ref{subsec:validation-methodology}) shows it captures only part of legal correctness, so primary conclusions rest on dual-judge faithfulness, citation-graph recovery and safety rates.
    \item \textbf{Safety Rejection and Robustness Rate (\%):} the share of Category~C and~D queries correctly neutralized. For Category~D we separate the Explicit Safety Refusal Rate ($R_{\text{ref}}$), the Verified Substantive Refutation Rate ($R_{\text{refut}}$, refuting the false premise without fatal error), the Total Safe Handling Rate ($R_{\text{safe}}=R_{\text{ref}}\cup R_{\text{refut}}$), the Fatal Error Rate ($R_{\text{fatal}}$, validating false or overruled doctrine) and the Uncommitted Hedge Rate ($R_{\text{hedge}}$).
    \item \textbf{Stage Latency (ms):} mean $\pm$ SD time for routing, retrieval, NLI grading and generation across all runs.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Validation Methodology and Component Ablation Design}
\label{subsec:validation-methodology}
The protocol incorporates four validation controls:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Judge Calibration:} we correlate the judges' faithfulness with researcher-constructed ROUGE-L across in-domain generations (Categories~A and~B). To handle repeated evaluations within queries, the primary analysis uses whole-query cluster bootstrap and query-cluster permutation over $N_q=\JudgeCalibN$ clusters ($N_{\text{obs}}=\JudgeCalibNObs$ valid pairs): Pearson $r=\JudgePearsonR$ (95\% bootstrap CI $\JudgePearsonCI$; permutation $p=\frac{k+1}{P+1}=\JudgePearsonP$ with $P=10{,}000$ and $k=0$ exceedances; Spearman $\rho=\JudgeSpearmanRho, p=\JudgeSpearmanP$; Kendall $\tau=\JudgeKendallTau, p=\JudgeKendallP$). Collapsing to query means changes the estimand to between-query variation, giving $r_{\text{mean}}=\JudgeMeanPearsonR$ (Fisher 95\% CI $\JudgeMeanPearsonCI$, $p=\JudgeMeanPearsonP$). Historical figures ($r=\HistoricalJudgePearsonR$, pilot $r=\HistoricalPilotJudgePearsonR$) were hard-coded without machine-readable provenance and failed reproduction across 384 candidate preprocessing configurations; the reported estimates come from the corrected canonical analysis. On the 35-query pilot ($N=525$ runs over 3 pipelines and 5 repeats; $N=260$ valid in-domain pairs over 20 query clusters) $r=\PilotJudgePearsonR$ ($p=\PilotJudgePearsonP$). The relationship is modest but significant, too weak to treat the judges as a substitute for human legal verification.
    \item \textbf{Multi-Run Provenance:} dates are file-modification times or logged runner headers. Baseline generations ($1{,}400$, \texttt{llama-3.3-70b-instruct}, no router or NLI) ran on 5--6 September 2026; the deployed TERRA run ($350$; router \texttt{gemma-3-12b}, NLI grader and generator \texttt{llama-3.3-70b-instruct}) and the dual-judge scoring of all $1{,}750$ answers (judges \texttt{qwen3-next-80b} and \texttt{gpt-oss-120b}) on 14 September 2026, with four concurrent workers (36 of 350 queries hit HTTP~429, 620\,s of server-imposed sleep). All calls used $T{=}0.0$, which does not guarantee identical outputs on Databricks Model Serving: a second same-day TERRA run gave identical answers on $\TerraIdenticalAnswerFraction$ of queries overall ($\TerraIdenticalAnswerCatA$ on Category~A, $\TerraIdenticalAnswerCatB$ on B, $\TerraIdenticalAnswerCatC$ on C, $\TerraIdenticalAnswerCatD$ on D); the low Category~B figure mainly reflects that the earlier run predates the router update, so for the 241 queries forced to HARD there, execution paths differed. The pilot ablation was scored by a single earlier judge whose identity and date are not recorded, so its scores are not comparable with the dual-judge scores. CaseFacts, CLERC and the obscure suite used the same two judges (identifiers recorded in every raw row for CaseFacts and CLERC); the temporal probe (17 September) also used two judges but does not record their identifiers. The Category~D certification (20 September, $N=75$) used the Llama-3.3-70B semantic verifier, CaseFacts and CLERC ran on 18 September ($N=170$ each), and the obscure suite and famous contrast on 19--20 September ($N=120$ and $76$). These four use task-specific rubrics, so their scores are not on the 1,750-run faithfulness scale, and the dates of the first three are file-modification times.
    \item \textbf{Component-Level Ablation Matrix:} three same-architecture ablations accompany \textbf{Full TERRA} (Router + EEG Traversal + NLI Smart Grader): \textbf{Ablation~1} replaces graph traversal with Flat Vector RAG while keeping NLI grading; \textbf{Ablation~2} disables entailment verification and self-correction; and \textbf{Ablation~3} forces all queries through the full traversal path.
    \item \textbf{Hyperparameter Freezing and Benchmark Isolation:} retrieval and graph parameters ($k=\IssueTwentyFourSeedK$, $d=\IssueTwentyFourBfsDepth$, hub cap $\IssueTwentyFourHubCap$, $\tau=\IssueTwentyFourDistThreshold$) were frozen before benchmark construction on the basis of topological constraints and feasibility testing on an independent $N=\IssueTwentyFourDevQueryCount$-query pilot (\IssueTwentyFourDevDate; \IssueTwentyFourDevTotalEvals\ run records over \IssueTwentyFourDevPipelineCount\ pipelines and \IssueTwentyFourDevRunCount\ stability runs). An automated audit verifies chronological precedence, zero query ID and prompt-text overlap (\IssueTwentyFourOverlapPct), and no query-text or reference-answer contamination in ChromaDB across all \IssueTwentyFourTestQueryCount\ queries.
\end{enumerate}

