\subsection{Data and Code Availability}
\label{subsec:data-code-availability}
\label{subsec:reproducibility}
All benchmark data, code and model specifications are released under an open-source license at \texttt{https://github.com/zaforsaadik7/TERRA-Temporal-Evolution-and-Reasoning-Trace-RAG}, with archival snapshots on Zenodo upon publication. The package includes: (1)~the 350-query benchmark (\texttt{terra\_350\_benchmark.json}, SHA256: \texttt{8144fa81...}) with gold categories, doctrine labels and reference answers; (2)~the 19 doctrine-invalidation pairs and 47 obscure overruled pairs; (3)~the 20,000-case EEG index (\texttt{terra\_eeg\_index\_20k.json}, SHA256: \texttt{\IssueTwentyThreeActiveCorpusHashShort}, \IssueTwentyThreeActiveEdges\ edges: \IssueTwentyThreeActiveCites\ \texttt{CITES}, \IssueTwentyThreeActiveOverrules\ \texttt{OVERRULES}) with all 20,000 reasoning traces, the construction pipeline (\texttt{scripts/ingest\_20k\_eeg\_and\_vector\_db.py}) and the edge list (\texttt{data/terra\_eeg\_edges\_145k.tsv}); (4)~execution and evaluation scripts (\texttt{ask\_terra.py}, \texttt{eval\_terra.py}); (5)~verbatim prompt templates and Pydantic schemas; (6)~frozen configurations (\texttt{configs/terra\_config.json}); (7)~the dual-judge evaluation records ($N=\numTotalEvals$, \texttt{data/terra\_canonical\_v2\_eval.json}, SHA256: \texttt{95e775e2...}) and the CaseFacts, CLERC, obscure-suite and NLI logs in \texttt{data/}; and (8)~table scripts (\texttt{Paper/scripts/generate\_stats.py}) regenerating every table and macro constant. Raw corpora and vector stores are reconstructible via the ingestion pipeline. \textbf{Models:} generation and the NLI grader use \texttt{meta-llama/Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct} (\texttt{databricks-meta-llama-3-3-70b-instruct}, $T=0.0$, max tokens~4,096); the router uses Gemma 3 12B Instruction-tuned (\texttt{\RouterModel}, $T=0.0$, Pydantic validation); dense retrieval uses \texttt{sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2} (\TraceEmbeddingDimensions\ dimensions, Euclidean $L_2$, ChromaDB PersistentClient, top-$k=2$, $d_{L2} \le 1.3$); and judging uses the two judge models of Section~\ref{subsec:eval-metrics} under the frozen rubric. Local retrieval ran on Windows 11 Enterprise (16 cores, 64~GB RAM, ChromaDB v\TraceChromaVersion, NetworkX v\TraceNetworkXVersion, Python 3.11) and remote inference on NVIDIA A100-SXM4-80GB clusters.
