\subsubsection{Benchmark Protocol and Provenance Audit}
\label{subsubsec:clerc-protocol}
\textbf{Track~1} ($N=\ClercNQueries$) is a stratified sample of 120 pre-1980 drafting contexts from the 1,498 candidate instances in the CLERC test split, drawn before observing any model output with a fixed seed (\texttt{SEED=42}), balanced between 60 Direct Precedent Citation and 60 Indirect Synthesis queries across five eras (1850--1880: $11.7\%$; 1881--1910: $20.0\%$; 1911--1940: $26.7\%$; 1941--1960: $21.7\%$; 1961--1980: $20.0\%$). \textbf{Track~2} ($N=\ClercNRefusal$) is not a native CLERC task but a TERRA-specific refusal stress test of 50 negative controls (39 post-1980 Supreme Court decisions, 11 non-SCOTUS federal circuit or statutory queries). An automated provenance audit found $0/120$ Track~1 targets among the 34 hand-curated seed nodes (overlapping queries were purged), so evaluation is not aided by the bespoke traces; it does not preclude retrieval over ordinary opinions in the shared 20,000-opinion corpus. The broader holding-extraction cascade (Tier~1 syllabi and Tier~2 headnotes, $51.1\%$ of opinions) was not separately screened. The harness also asserts, before each batch, that the NLI grader short-circuits three known-refusal probes. All systems used strict payload parity ($T=0.0$, max tokens 4096, identical LLaMA 3.3 70B generator) and the two non-LLaMA judges (\texttt{qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct}, \texttt{gpt-oss-120b}); citation precision and recall come from deterministic \texttt{eyecite} parsing.

\subsubsection{Retrieval Hyperparameter Robustness and Traversal Horizon Sensitivity}
\label{subsubsec:hyperparameter-sensitivity}
Table~\ref{tab:retrieval_eval} (Panel~C) sweeps \IssueTwentySixConfigCount\ configurations on the $N=\CatBRetrievalQueries$ Category~B queries. The unconstrained pre-Issue~\#13 engine reproduces the published baseline ($k=\IssueTwentyFourSeedK, d=\IssueTwentyFourBfsDepth, C=\IssueTwentyFourHubCap$) with 100/100 bit-identical parity (\IssueTwentySixHistMacro\ macro, \IssueTwentySixHistFullPath\ full-path recall). Truncating to $d=1$ cuts full-path recovery to \IssueTwentySixHistDepthOnePath\ (\IssueTwentySixHistDepthOneMacro\ macro) and context from \IssueTwentySixHistMeanChars\ to \IssueTwentySixHistDepthOneChars\ characters (latency \IssueTwentySixHistOfflineLatencyMS~ms to \IssueTwentySixHistDepthOneLatencyMS~ms); $d=3$ adds modest recovery (\IssueTwentySixHistDepthThreePath, 68/100) at much larger context (\IssueTwentySixHistDepthThreeChars\ characters, \IssueTwentySixHistDepthThreeLatencyMS~ms). A hub cap of $C=25$ restricts recovery (\IssueTwentySixHistCapTwentyFivePath), while $C=100$ nearly completes it (\IssueTwentySixHistCapOneHundredPath, \IssueTwentySixHistCapOneHundredMacro\ macro) at \IssueTwentySixHistCapOneHundredChars\ characters. Adaptive depth matches static $d=2$ (\IssueTwentySixHistAdaptiveMacro\ macro, \IssueTwentySixHistAdaptivePath\ path), single-seed initialization ($k=1^\ast$) reaches \IssueTwentySixHistKOnePath\ path recovery, and vector-seed expansion ($k=4^\ast$) retrieves no seeds because of an active-index category filter (\IssueTwentySixHistKFourPath). Under the production caps ($35{,}000$ structural characters, 40 traversed nodes, asymmetric hub budgets), traversal stops after a mean of \IssueTwentySixBoundMeanVisitedNodes\ expansion centers and assembles \IssueTwentySixBoundMeanFullNodes\ authorities; the bounded engine keeps \IssueTwentySixGoldRetentionPct\ of gold landmarks (\IssueTwentySixGoldRetentionCount) and \IssueTwentySixBoundFullPath\ full-path recall (48/63 on 3-node, 12/32 on 4-node, 1/5 on 5-node chains), cutting context