\subsection{Data and Code Availability}
\label{subsec:data-code-availability}
\label{subsec:reproducibility}
To enable independent replication and adhere to open-science standards, all benchmark data, experimental code, and model specifications are publicly released under an open-source license via our public repository at \texttt{https://github.com/zaforsaadik7/TERRA-Temporal-Evolution-and-Reasoning-Trace-RAG}, with permanent archival release snapshots deposited on Zenodo upon publication. The public reproducibility package includes: (1)~the complete 350-query benchmark suite (\texttt{terra\_350\_benchmark.json}, SHA256: \texttt{8144fa81...}) with gold categories, doctrine labels, and reference answers; (2)~the 19 verified doctrine-invalidation pairs and 47 obscure overruled pairs; (3)~the 20,000-case Event Evolution Graph index (\texttt{terra\_eeg\_index\_20k.json}, SHA256: \texttt{\IssueTwentyThreeActiveCorpusHashShort}, \IssueTwentyThreeActiveEdges\ directed edges: \IssueTwentyThreeActiveCites\ \texttt{CITES} and \IssueTwentyThreeActiveOverrules\ authoritative \texttt{OVERRULES}), containing all 20,000 procedural reasoning traces, alongside the graph construction pipeline (\texttt{scripts/ingest\_20k\_eeg\_and\_vector\_db.py}) and standalone citation edge list (\texttt{data/terra\_eeg\_edges\_145k.tsv}); (4)~complete execution and evaluation scripts (\texttt{ask\_terra.py}, \texttt{eval\_terra.py}); (5)~verbatim system prompt templates and structured Pydantic schemas; (6)~system configurations and frozen hyperparameters (\texttt{configs/terra\_config.json}); (7)~canonical dual-judge evaluation records ($N=\numTotalEvals$ evaluations, \texttt{data/terra\_canonical\_v2\_eval.json}, SHA256: \texttt{95e775e2...}) alongside specialized benchmark logs (CaseFacts, CLERC, Obscure Overruled, and NLI verification suites in \texttt{data/}); and (8)~automated table scripts (\texttt{Paper/scripts/generate\_stats.py}) regenerating every table (Tables~I--XXIII) and macro constant. Large raw corpora (unparsed text and vector stores) are reconstructible via the ingestion pipeline. \textbf{Component Model Specifications:} Generative inference and the NLI Smart Grader employ \texttt{meta-llama/Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct} (Databricks Model Serving API: \texttt{databricks-meta-llama-3-3-70b-instruct}, $T=0.0$, max tokens~4,096). The Traffic Cop intent router uses Google Gemma 3 12B Instruction-tuned (API: \texttt{\RouterModel}, $T=0.0$, Pydantic schema validation; Table~\ref{tab:router_eval}). Dense semantic retrieval uses \texttt{sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2} (\TraceEmbeddingDimensions-dimensional vectors, Euclidean $L_2$ metric, ChromaDB PersistentClient, top-$k=2$, $d_{L2} \le 1.3$). Independent critic scoring utilizes \texttt{databricks-qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct} (Judge~1) and \texttt{databricks-gpt-oss-120b} (Judge~2) under the frozen rubric (Table~\ref{tab:run_provenance}). Local retrieval executed on Windows 11 Enterprise (16 cores, 64~GB RAM, ChromaDB v\TraceChromaVersion, NetworkX v\TraceNetworkXVersion, Python 3.11); remote inference on NVIDIA A100-SXM4-80GB GPU clusters.

The ablation results confirm three fundamental architectural insights:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Architectural Necessity of EEG Graph Traversal:} Disabling multi-hop citation graph traversal (Ablation 1) drops evolutionary reasoning faithfulness from $\AblationFullCatBFaithfulness$ to $\AblationOneCatBFaithfulness$ (a relative reduction of $\AblationEEGFaithfulnessRelDrop$). Semantic vector retrieval over isolated reasoning traces fails to capture the chronological directionality of legal precedents; when retrieved text segments lack explicit bridge citations connecting non-contiguous decisions, generative synthesis degrades.
    \item \textbf{Critical Function of the NLI Smart Grader:} Disabling the semantic entailment gate (Ablation 2) removes the system's defensive verification firewall, dropping adversarial safety rejection on Category D from $\AblationFullCatDSafetyRate$ to $\AblationTwoCatDSafetyRate$. Without the autonomous entailment audit, the generator credulously complies with deceptive queries regarding overruled precedent.
    \item \textbf{Operational Efficiency of Traffic Cop Routing:} Bypassing the SLM complexity router (Ablation 3) forces straightforward single-point factual lookups through heavy multi-hop BFS graph expansion and NLI grading, increasing execution latency on Category A queries from $\AblationFullLatencyMedianSeconds\text{ s}$ to $\AblationThreeLatencyMedianSeconds\text{ s}$ ($\AblationRouterLatencySlowdownFactor\times$ slowdown) while introducing $\AblationThreeCatAFalseRefusalCount$ ($\AblationThreeCatAFalseRefusalRate$) false refusals due to context pollution from extraneous citing precedents. Notably, Ablation 3 reports a marginally higher Category B faithfulness ($\AblationThreeCatBFaithfulness$) than Full TERRA ($\AblationFullCatBFaithfulness$): because the forced hard path enforces BFS graph traversal for every query without exception, it eliminates any residual router misclassification of borderline evolutionary queries, yielding a minor uplift on multi-hop reasoning. However, the operational cost---a $\AblationRouterLatencySlowdownFactor\times$ latency increase and a $\AblationThreeCatAFalseRefusalRate$ false refusal rate on Category A factual queries---makes the unrouted configuration disadvantageous for latency-sensitive applications. The router provides critical operational triage, enabling fast sub-two-second execution on direct factual inquiries while reserving graph traversal for complex multi-decade doctrines.
\end{enumerate}

