Key empirical findings include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Dual-Metric Faithfulness Leadership:} Distinguishing overall faithfulness (awarding perfect scores to epistemic refusals) from answer-only faithfulness (evaluating generated legal arguments) is critical. TERRA attains the highest overall faithfulness ($\TerraOverallFaithfulnessMean$, 95\% CI $\TerraOverallCI$) ahead of HippoRAG-Adapted ($\HippoRAGOverallFaithfulnessMean$, $\HippoRAGOverallCI$), whose confidence intervals overlap, and the highest answer-only faithfulness ($\TerraAnswerOnlyFaithfulnessMean$ vs.\ $\HippoRAGAnswerOnlyFaithfulnessMean$ for HippoRAG); answer-only scores are conditional on different answered subsets (TERRA answers $\TerraAnsweredQueries$/350 queries, HippoRAG \HippoRAGAnswerOnlyCount, Flat \FlatRAGAnswerOnlyCount, GraphRAG \GraphRAGAnswerOnlyCount). Flagged refusals total $\TerraFlaggedRefusals$/350 for TERRA against $\HippoRAGFlaggedRefusals$ for HippoRAG, $\FlatRAGFlaggedRefusals$ for Flat and $\GraphRAGFlaggedRefusals$ for GraphRAG ($\TerraEpistemicRefusals$, $\HippoEpistemicRefusals$, $\FlatEpistemicRefusals$ and $\GraphEpistemicRefusals$ counting unflagged textual refusals).
    \item \textbf{Evolutionary Multi-Hop Performance (Category B):} On multi-decade precedent trajectories, TERRA achieves $\TerraCatBFaithfulness$ faithfulness, significantly outperforming all evaluated retrieval baselines: HippoRAG ($\HippoRAGCatBFaithfulness$, $\Delta=\CatBTerraVsHippoRAGDelta$, $q=\CatBTerraVsHippoRAGAdjQ$), GraphRAG ($\GraphRAGCatBFaithfulness$, $\Delta=\CatBTerraVsGraphRAGDelta$, $q=\CatBTerraVsGraphRAGAdjQ$), and Flat Vector RAG ($\FlatRAGCatBFaithfulness$, $\Delta=\CatBTerraVsFlatDelta$, $q=\CatBTerraVsFlatRAGAdjQ$). A non-retrieval Direct LLM scores higher on Category~B ($\DirectLLMCatBFaithfulness$, $\Delta=\CatBTerraVsDirectLLMDelta$, $q=\CatBTerraVsDirectLLMAdjQ$; TERRA win rate $\TerraVsDirectLLMCatBWinRate$), consistent with dual judges rewarding fluent ungrounded parametric prose over grounded retrieval constraints, whereas TERRA's retrieval evaluation shows stronger Category~B citation coverage and full-precedent-path recovery.
    \item \textbf{Factual Lookup Performance (Category A):} On Category~A, TERRA scores $\TerraCatAFaithfulness$ against HippoRAG $\HippoRAGCatAFaithfulness$, Flat $\FlatRAGCatAFaithfulness$ and Direct LLM $\DirectLLMCatAFaithfulness$; it is significantly higher than Direct ($\CatATerraVsDirectLLMDelta$) and Flat ($\CatATerraVsFlatRAGDelta$, $q=\CatATerraVsFlatRAGAdjQ$) and statistically indistinguishable from GraphRAG ($\CatATerraVsGraphRAGDelta$, $q=\CatATerraVsGraphRAGAdjQ$) and HippoRAG ($\CatATerraVsHippoRAGDelta$, $q=\CatATerraVsHippoRAGAdjQ$). The safety gate falsely refuses $\TerraCatAFalseRefusals$/100 Category~A queries (and $\TerraCatBFalseRefusals$/100 on Category~B; see Limitation~\ref{item:falserefusal}).
    \item \textbf{Adversarial Deception Robustness (Category D):} On deceptive prompts embedding authentic titles with fabricated premises (e.g., maritime quotas in \textit{Smith v. Allwright}), TERRA achieves $\mathbf{\TerraCatDRejection}$ explicit safety refusals alongside $\TerraCatDRefutationRate$ verified substantive refutations ($\TerraCatDRefutations$), delivering $\mathbf{\TerraCatDSafeHandlingRate}$ total safe handling ($\TerraCatDSafeHandling$; Table~\ref{tab:refusal_breakdown}, Panel~B). In paired binary contingency testing across the 75 matched adversarial queries, TERRA demonstrates $100\%$ monotonic containment over all baseline-safe completions ($c=0$ across all four comparative baselines: every query safely resolved by any baseline is also safely resolved by TERRA). On total safe handling, TERRA achieves a statistically significant advantage over GraphRAG-Adapted ($\GraphRAGCatDSafeHandling \to \TerraCatDSafeHandling$, $b=6, c=0$, exact two-tailed McNemar $p = \TerraVsGraphRAGCatDSafeMcNemarP$) while outperforming Flat Vector RAG ($\FlatRAGCatDSafeHandling$, $b=5, c=0$, $p = \TerraVsFlatRAGCatDSafeMcNemarP$), HippoRAG-Adapted ($\HippoRAGCatDSafeHandling$, $b=3, c=0$, $p = \TerraVsHippoRAGCatDSafeMcNemarP$), and Direct LLM ($\DirectLLMCatDSafeHandling$, $b=2, c=0$, $p = \TerraVsDirectLLMCatDSafeMcNemarP$). On explicit epistemic refusal, TERRA dominates Direct LLM ($\TerraCatDRejection$ vs.\ $\DirectLLMCatDRejection$, $b=48, c=0$, $p = \TerraVsDirectLLMCatDRefusalMcNemarP$, $+\TerraCatDRejectionRate$), while achieving the highest refusal rate across all evaluated architectures (vs.\ Flat RAG $\FlatRAGCatDRejectionRate$, $p = \TerraVsFlatRAGCatDRefusalMcNemarP$; HippoRAG $\HippoRAGCatDRejectionRate$, $p = \TerraVsHippoRAGCatDRefusalMcNemarP$; GraphRAG $\GraphRAGCatDRejectionRate$, $p = \TerraVsGraphRAGCatDRefusalMcNemarP$). Under calibrated dual-budget traversal and fast-path grounding, fatal constraint violations were observed on only $\TerraCatDFatalErrors$, with $\TerraCatDHedges$ uncommitted hedges. The only two remaining fatal errors (queries D033 and D034) are reciprocal procedural misattributions between closely coupled apportionment landmarks (\textit{Baker v. Carr} [1962] and \textit{Reynolds v. Sims} [1964]), where the generator transposed the threshold justiciability holding with the substantive apportionment standard. Category~D labels come from a calibrated semantic verifier served by the same model family as the generator (Limitation~\ref{item:samefamily}).
    \item \textbf{Out-of-Context Safety (Category C):} On queries demonstrably outside indexed U.S. Supreme Court case law (spanning natural sciences, foreign law, municipal ordinances, contemporary software, and corporate accounting), TERRA achieves a $\mathbf{\TerraCatCRejectionRate}$ safety refusal rate ($\mathbf{\TerraCatCRejection}$), tying GraphRAG ($\mathbf{\GraphRAGCatCRejection}$) and outperforming Flat RAG ($\FlatRAGCatCRejection$) and Direct LLM ($\DirectLLMCatCRejection$).
