\subsubsection{Standalone Intent Classification Performance}
As reported in Table~\ref{tab:router_eval} (Panel~A), the Traffic Cop Router achieves high standalone classification fidelity:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{High Overall Accuracy and Balanced $F_1$:} Across the full 350-query suite, the router achieves an overall accuracy of $\mathbf{\RouterAccuracy}$ ($\RouterCorrectCount$) and a macro-averaged $F_1$ score of $\mathbf{\RouterMacroFOne}$ (macro precision $\RouterMacroPrecision$, macro recall $\RouterMacroRecall$). On the factual class (Gold EASY, Category~A), the classifier achieves $\RouterEasyFOne$ $F_1$ with perfect recall ($\RouterEasyRecall$, $100/100$). On complex inquiries (Gold HARD, Categories~B, C, and D), it achieves $\RouterHardFOne$ $F_1$ with $\RouterHardPrecision$ precision ($240/240$). All \RouterErrorCount\ misroutes are Category~D queries sent to the fast path, where NLI grading is bypassed; nevertheless all ten were safely handled (8 flagged refusals and 2 unflagged textual refusals, D068 and D070; none affirmed a fatal error).
    \item \textbf{Asymmetric Error Profile Favoring Safety:} The directional error profile reflects deliberate architectural alignment. The False-HARD rate on factual queries is certified at $\mathbf{\RouterFalseHardRate}$ ($0/100$), ensuring that single-point factual lookups are never needlessly penalized by multi-hop graph expansion or context flooding. Across all complex queries, the False-EASY rate is $\mathbf{\RouterFalseEasyRate}$ ($10/250$), with zero false-EASY routes on Category~B multi-hop evolutionary chains ($0/100$) and Category~C out-of-context unanswerable probes ($0/75$).
    \item \textbf{Standalone Inference Latency Profile:} In isolated execution, the Gemma 3 12B router operates with a median wall-clock latency of $\mathbf{\RouterLatencyMedianMs\text{ ms}}$ and a mean of $\RouterLatencyMeanMs\text{ ms}$ (p90 of $\RouterLatencyPNinetyMs\text{ ms}$, spanning from a minimum of $\RouterLatencyMinMs\text{ ms}$ to an un-cached cold start maximum of $\RouterLatencyMaxMs\text{ ms}$). This lightweight footprint enables responsive front-line complexity triage before invoking heavier 70B parameter models.
\end{itemize}

