\subsubsection{Four-Layer Scientific Evaluation Architecture}
\label{subsubsec:four-layer-eval}
To establish methodological clarity and prevent confounding between internal mechanism isolation, standardized retrieval benchmarks, and external generalization scope, our empirical validation adheres to a structured four-layer evaluation architecture:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Layer 1: Internal Controlled Benchmark ($N=\numUniqueQueries$):} Isolates fine-grained algorithmic mechanisms (temporal citation graphs, procedural reasoning traces, NLI entailment gate) across four controlled experimental categories (Sections~\ref{subsec:safety-and-factual}--\ref{subsec:ablation-study}).
    \item \textbf{Layer 2: Standardized Precedent Retrieval and Generative Legal Drafting (CLERC Sample, NAACL 2025):} Evaluates general-purpose precedent case retrieval (Task 1A) and unredacted judicial analysis generation (Task 1B) on external, uncurated judicial opinions from a stratified sample of CLERC ($N=120$), complemented by an engineered out-of-corpus refusal stress test ($N=50$).
    \item \textbf{Layer 3: External Temporal Fact-Checking and Overruling Recognition (CaseFacts, ACL 2026):} Evaluates temporal proposition classification, doctrinal overruling recognition, and anti-circularity under third-party adjudication ($N=120$ in-corpus, $N=50$ modern refusal controls; Section~\ref{subsec:casefacts-generalization}).
    \item \textbf{Layer 4: Licensed Legal Practitioner Evaluation:} Parked as essential future work (Section~\ref{subsec:limitations}, Limitation~1) due to resource constraints; all evaluations at Layers 1--3 rely on reproducible algorithmic and non-LLaMA dual-judge protocols.
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{Benchmark Protocol, Provenance Hardening, and Anti-Leakage Audit}
\label{subsubsec:clerc-protocol}
The evaluation protocol is divided into two distinct tracks:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Track 1: Standardized CLERC Precedent Retrieval and Generative Drafting Sample ($N=\ClercNQueries$):} To evaluate on external common-law contexts while maintaining reproducible execution, we extracted a stratified sample of 120 authentic pre-1980 legal drafting contexts from the 1,498 candidate instances in the CLERC test split. The sample was drawn pre-hoc (prior to observing any model outputs) with a fixed random seed (\texttt{SEED=42}), balanced equally between 60 Direct Precedent Citation queries and 60 Indirect Synthesis queries across five historical eras (1850--1880: $11.7\%$; 1881--1910: $20.0\%$; 1911--1940: $26.7\%$; 1941--1960: $21.7\%$; 1961--1980: $20.0\%$).
    \item \textbf{Track 2: TERRA-Specific Modern and Cross-Jurisdictional Refusal Stress Test ($N=\ClercNRefusal$):} Rather than a native CLERC benchmark task, Track 2 serves as a dedicated architectural safety stress test comprising 50 negative control queries (39 post-1980 modern Supreme Court decisions and 11 non-SCOTUS federal circuit/statutory queries) to rigorously evaluate epistemic boundary enforcement and anti-hallucination guardrails under out-of-corpus pressure.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Empirical Findings: Precedent Retrieval and Analysis Generation (Track 1)}
As detailed in Table~\ref{tab:clerc_eval} (Panel A), Full TERRA demonstrates decisive structural advantages across retrieval and generation while surfacing a clear architectural boundary in open-ended generative drafting:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Precedent Retrieval Comparison (Task 1A):} On the evaluated CLERC sample, Full TERRA achieved slightly higher Recall@10 ($\ClercTerraRecallTen$ vs. $\ClercFlatRecallTen$) and nDCG@10 ($\ClercTerraNDCG$ vs. $\ClercFlatNDCG$) than Flat Vector RAG, alongside an MRR@10 of $\ClercTerraMRR$ vs. $\ClercFlatMRR$ for dense retrieval. While directional improvements are observed from chronological citation traversal and holding re-ranking, the absolute margin on single-turn benchmark queries remains modest ($\ClercTerraRecallTenDelta$ percentage points Recall@10).
    \item \textbf{Generative Analysis and Lexical Fidelity (Task 1B):} In judicial analysis drafting, TERRA attains $\ClercTerraRougeL$ ROUGE-L and $\ClercTerraBleu$ BLEU-4, reflecting structural adherence to judicial reasoning styles without degenerating into verbatim repetition. Deterministic \texttt{eyecite} parse-tree extraction demonstrates that TERRA attains the highest Citation Precision among evaluated systems ($\text{CP} = \ClercTerraCitePrecision$, vs. $\ClercFlatCitePrecision$ for Flat Vector RAG and $\ClercDirectCitePrecision$ for Direct LLM) alongside a Citation Recall of $\text{CR} = \ClercTerraCiteRecall$ and Citation $F_1$ of $\ClercTerraCiteFOne$ (compared to Flat RAG's $\ClercFlatCiteRecall$ and Direct LLM's $\ClercDirectCiteRecall$, where Direct LLM's higher recall is driven by prolific ungrounded citation dumping).
    \item \textbf{Zero Case Caption Fabrication:} Forensic auditing of all citations extracted across the 120 Track 1 completions confirms that no fabricated case captions or phantom citations were observed in the audited TERRA outputs ($0/120$, $\ClercTerraFabricationTrackOne$). Every citation generated by TERRA corresponded to a verified historical Supreme Court authority ($\ClercTerraInCorpusCitePct$) or an authentic external context case ($\ClercTerraExternalCitePct$). When the NLI entailment gate fired to intercept drafting queries ($53/120$ cases), forensic auditing confirmed zero fabricated case citations among the short-circuited completions ($0/53$). By contrast, unaugmented Direct LLM suffered a $\ClercDirectFabricationTrackOne$ phantom citation rate, frequently inventing non-existent case captions and reporter volumes.
    \item \textbf{Disclosed Limitation: Doctrinal Misapplication Under Retrieval Horizon Truncation:} However, when generation proceeded on incomplete retrieval subgraphs ($67/120$ cases), independent dual judging flagged a doctrinal misapplication rate of $\ClercTerraMisapplicationTrackOne$ ($\ClercTerraMisapplicationOrTrackOne$ under Logical OR union, $\ClercTerraMisapplicationAndTrackOne$ under Unanimous AND consensus; compared to $\ClercFlatMisapplicationTrackOne$ for Flat Vector RAG and $\ClercDirectMisapplicationTrackOne$ for Direct LLM). Case-by-case auditing reveals that these errors are not phantom fabrications, but instances of doctrinal misapplication where the generator attempted to reason by analogy from retrieved historical authorities when the gold controlling precedent lay beyond the 2-hop traversal radius. This establishes a fundamental empirical principle: \textit{authentic evidence does not equate to sufficient evidence}. Grounding generations on authentic graph subgraphs completely eliminates phantom case inventions, but generative reasoning remains vulnerable to doctrinal error whenever relevant authority is truncated by the graph retrieval horizon. This finding directly motivates our in-depth examination of precedent-chain path recovery and multi-hop retrieval depth in Section~\ref{subsec:retrieval-quality}.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Empirical Findings: Refusal Control on Negative Probes (Track 2)}
On Track 2 (Table~\ref{tab:clerc_eval}, Panel B), Full TERRA establishes robust epistemic boundary discipline on external out-of-corpus queries:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Total Out-of-Corpus Discrimination:} TERRA achieves $\mathbf{\ClercTerraRefusalTrackTwo}$ total safe refusal on out-of-corpus queries, outperforming Flat Vector RAG ($\ClercFlatRefusalTrackTwo$) and Direct LLM ($\ClercDirectRefusalTrackTwo$) by a $3.3\times$ margin. We emphasize that Track 2 functions specifically as an engineered epistemic boundary and refusal stress test rather than an overall task utility metric. While high safe refusal ($\ClercTerraRefusalTrackTwo$) demonstrates effective containment of out-of-corpus queries, real-world deployment requires balancing safe refusal with retrieval coverage.
    \item \textbf{Hard NLI Interception and Gate Safety:} The NLI entailment gate successfully intercepted $\mathbf{\ClercTerraHardRefusalTrackTwo}$ of out-of-corpus queries prior to generation. Forensic auditing confirmed zero fabricated case citations among the 86 gate-triggering events across both tracks (0/53 in Track 1, 0/33 in Track 2), confirming that gate interception reliably halts generation before unverified citations can be introduced.
    \item \textbf{Fabrication vs. Misapplication on Negative Probes:} Full TERRA recorded a certified $\mathbf{\ClercTerraFabricationTrackTwo}$ case caption fabrication rate on Track 2 (zero phantom case names across all 50 negative controls), compared to $\ClercDirectFabricationTrackTwo$ citation fabrication in Direct LLM. On the remaining 17 cases where generation proceeded without gate short-circuit (representing \ClercTerraMisapplicationOrTrackTwo\ across all 50 Track-2 queries; 100.0\% of generated completions), both judges unanimously flagged doctrinal misapplication ($\ClercTerraMisapplicationTrackTwo$), as the model attempted to answer modern post-1980 or statutory questions using historical pre-1980 SCOTUS authorities.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Overall Precedent-Chain Recovery Performance}
As reported in Table~\ref{tab:retrieval_eval} (Panel A), comparative retrievers exhibit fundamental divergences in their ability to capture multi-hop common-law trajectories:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Dense Semantic Search Horizon Truncation:} Flat Vector RAG achieves only $\CatBFlatMacroNodeRecall$ macro node recall ($\CatBFlatMicroNodeRecall$ micro) and captures the complete precedent trajectory on only $\CatBFlatFullPathRecall$ of queries ($\CatBFlatFullPathCount$). While dense embeddings successfully identify one or two lexically adjacent landmark cases, they almost completely fail to recover intermediate stepping-stone authorities across multi-decade evolutionary spans.
    \item \textbf{Static Community and Undirected Diffusion Limitations:} GraphRAG-Adapted achieves $\CatBGraphRAGMacroNodeRecall$ node recall and $\CatBGraphRAGFullPathRecall$ full-path recovery ($\CatBGraphRAGFullPathCount$), bounded by the fact that static Louvain modularity communities group cases topically without preserving chronological precedence. HippoRAG-Adapted achieves substantially higher node recall ($\CatBHippoRAGMacroNodeRecall$), but attains only $\CatBHippoRAGFullPathRecall$ full-chain path recall ($\CatBHippoRAGFullPathCount$). Because Personalized PageRank diffuses undirected probability mass across dense citation clusters, it frequently activates high-degree peripheral precedents rather than following the strict temporal citation spine.
    \item \textbf{Directional Precedent Spine Traversal:} Full TERRA achieves $\CatBTerraMacroNodeRecall$ macro node recall ($\CatBTerraMicroNodeRecall$ micro) and recovers the complete evolutionary chain on $\CatBTerraFullPathCount$/100 queries, a $\CatBTerraVsFlatNodeRecallDelta$ node-recall lift and $\CatBTerraVsFlatPathRecallFactor$ path-recovery factor over Flat Vector RAG (which recovers $\CatBFlatFullPathCount$/100), and $\CatBTerraVsHippoNodeRecallDelta$ and $\CatBTerraVsHippoPathRecallFactor$ over HippoRAG. By traversing chronologically directed citation edges and re-ranking via substantive holding headnotes, TERRA preserves the continuous chain of legal authority necessary for sound doctrinal synthesis.
\end{itemize}

