\section{Introduction and Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:Introduction}
Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has rapidly evolved into a foundational architecture for knowledge-intensive artificial intelligence. By explicitly decoupling the parametric memory of Large Language Models (LLMs) from dynamic external knowledge bases, RAG enables real-time factual grounding and transparent source attribution without the prohibitive computational costs of continuous model retraining. In high-stakes, knowledge-intensive environments---where factual errors and hallucinations carry severe real-world consequences---the fidelity of the retrieved context is not merely an optimization preference, but a strict operational requirement. As enterprise applications scale, the early paradigm of naive ``retrieve-then-generate'' pipelines characterized by static vector databases and fixed-length document chunking has proven structurally inadequate.

\phantomsection\label{sec:related-work}
\section{System Architecture and Methodology}
\label{sec:methodology}
TERRA is a two-phase GraphRAG pipeline. Offline ingestion constructs a directed Event Evolution Graph over the pre-1980 Supreme Court corpus, extracts citation and overruling relations, and serializes procedural reasoning traces for semantic retrieval. Online inference uses an independent Traffic Cop router to select a fast factual path or a bounded multi-hop path; retrieved graph context is checked by the NLI Smart Grader before grounded generation. The complete ingestion, routing, traversal, prompt, and leakage-control protocol is preserved in Supplementary Section~\ref{sec:supp-methods}.

The deployed configuration uses frozen graph data, top-$k$ semantic trace retrieval, bounded BFS expansion, explicit provenance formatting, and structured JSON validation. Model roles, endpoint identifiers, and implementation safeguards remain available in the supplement; the main text retains the mechanisms needed to interpret the primary ablations and results.
